==================================================
TASK 3: Verify Reproducibility via Restart
==================================================
Cell 1:
x = 10

Cell 2:
print(x)

Kernel Restart Action:
Restarted the Jupyter kernel (via Kernel -> Restart Kernel in the notebook interface) to wipe all in-memory variables and execution history.

Before Re-running Cell 1:
Running print(x) in a new cell raises a NameError: name 'x' is not defined because the kernel memory was cleared and Cell 1 has not executed in the fresh session.

After Re-running Cell 1:
Once Cell 1 is executed again to define x = 10, running print(x) in the new cell prints 10.


==================================================
TASK 4: Export and Sanitize for Sharing
==================================================
Export to Python Script Command:
jupyter nbconvert --to script secrets_demo.ipynb

Clear Notebook Outputs Command:
jupyter nbconvert --clear-output --inplace secrets_demo.ipynb

Security Risk Explanation:
A .ipynb file stores cell outputs directly inside its JSON structure. Even if you delete or edit a cell later, previously printed secrets (like API keys, tokens, or credentials) can remain saved in the output fields or metadata. Sharing the notebook as-is exposes those secrets to anyone who opens the file or views it in version control, so clearing outputs before sharing helps prevent accidental credential leaks.
